# Mutation cases for "a transition with no target ends a microstep, not the
# macrostep" — the halves a cargo round can reach.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/targetless_transition_completes_macrostep.cases`.
#
# W3C SCXML Appendix D's main event loop returns to
# `selectEventlessTransitions()` after every microstep and drains the internal
# queue in the same inner loop, without asking whether the microstep moved the
# machine. It cannot ask: §scxml-3.13 defines a transition with no `target` as
# one that exits and enters nothing and runs its content in place, which is a
# microstep like any other. What that content assigns can enable an eventless
# transition, and what it raises lands on the internal queue; both belong to
# the same macrostep.
#
# Measured 2026-08-20, three of seven engines ended the macrostep there, in
# three shapes. Two of them are C++ and live in
# `targetless_transition_completes_macrostep_ctest.cases`. The third was the
# Kotlin CODE GENERATOR, and it is here: its eventless selection was emitted
# only for transitions that named a target, so a targetless eventless
# transition was dropped from selection while the transition-content dispatch
# still carried its content — actions the generated machine could never ask
# for.
#
# Since 2026-09-25 the Kotlin backend selects through the runtime's Appendix D
# transcription (`com.sce.runtime.Microstep`): the walk from each atomic state
# up through its ancestors, conflict removal and the exit and entry sets are
# written once there, and what the generator emits is the half only the
# document can answer — `firstEnabledTransition(state, event)`, the first of a
# state's OWN transitions an event enables, answered with a transition object
# declared once in the machine's companion. The cases below are the shipped
# defect restated against that surface: the transition not offered, offered as
# a state change, offered nowhere its ancestor can be reached from. The walk
# itself, and the macrostep carrying on past an in-place microstep, are the
# runtime's and run under Gradle (`MicrostepTest`, and the seven-channel
# fixture of the same name); cargo cannot reach them.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --test targetless_eventless_transition_is_selectable
mutation_runtime_targets tools/codegen/templates/kotlin/process_event.kt.jinja2 \
    tools/codegen/templates/kotlin/state_machine.kt.jinja2
mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/targetless_eventless_transition_is_selectable.rs

# ── The Kotlin generator: selection ────────────────────────────────
#
# The shipped filter, restored on the new surface. A state whose only eventless
# transition is targetless gets no arm, so the runtime's walk finds nothing to
# select from it — and the missing microstep is silent, because the content is
# still emitted under that transition's index in `executeTransitionContent`.

mutation_case "the selection offers only a transition that names a target" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/kotlin/process_event.kt.jinja2",
     """{% for trans in state.transitions %}
{% if not arm.closed %}""",
     """{% for trans in state.transitions %}
{% if not arm.closed and trans.targets %}""")
PY

# The same filter, narrowed to where the old one hid: a COMPOUND state's own
# targetless eventless transition. Appendix D reaches it from the leaf by
# walking up, so it has to be offered under the ancestor; a filter that dropped
# it there would leave every atomic state's arm intact and the leaf with
# nothing to inherit.

mutation_case "a compound state's targetless eventless transition is not offered" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/kotlin/process_event.kt.jinja2",
     """{% for trans in state.transitions %}
{% if not arm.closed %}""",
     """{% for trans in state.transitions %}
{% if not arm.closed and (trans.targets or not state.children) %}""")
PY

# ── The Kotlin generator: what the selection answers ────────────────
#
# A targetless transition answered as a transition to its own source is a
# state change: the runtime exits and re-enters the state, so `<onexit>` and
# `<onentry>` run for a transition the clause says leaves the configuration
# alone. The runtime reads "moves nothing" from an EMPTY target list, which the
# transition's declaration is the one place to spell.

mutation_case "a targetless transition is declared as a transition to its source" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/kotlin/state_machine.kt.jinja2",
     """{% else %}emptyList(){% endif %},""",
     """{% else %}listOf(StateTarget({{ machine_name }}State.{{ state_id | to_state_class_name }})){% endif %},""")
PY

# ── The `else` that Kotlin rejects ─────────────────────────────────
#
# `-Werror` refuses a redundant `else` on an exhaustive `when`, so a flag that
# always says "needed" turns every document whose states all have a
# transition into a compile failure. That is not hypothetical here: offering
# targetless transitions is what made such documents reachable at all.

mutation_case "the selection carries an else even when every state has a transition" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/kotlin/process_event.kt.jinja2",
     """{% if ns_selecting.states < model.states | length %}""",
     """{% if ns_selecting.states <= model.states | length %}""")
PY
